could the covered wave be nearing a peak  already well perhaps the latest data  from the office for National statistics  suggests just over 2 million people were  infected in the weekend in the 10th of  October that's one in 30 and a rise of  just over 15 on the week before but  there is a lag in this data and other  sources which are more up to date though  knowledge comprehensive suggests the  spread of the virus may be slowing  remember the daily dashboard nowhere  near as many people test these days so  it's no good for judging covered rates  but it's useful for tracking the trend  and it does seem to be leveling off  that's backed up by figures from the UK  Health Security Agency rates in hospital  patients and staff are down to 11 in 100  000 from just under 14 the week before  it suggests rates are coming down in the  wider Community too now if that trend is  firmed up by more data in the next week  or so it will come as a relief for the  NHS but hospitals are still facing a  serious surge of flu rates are up in  every age group according to the latest  data but there has been a particularly  sharp rise in children aged between 5  and 14. it's now about 11 and the UK  Health security agency has warned of an  increase in more severe illness in even  younger children these are  hospitalization rates and they're up in  every age group but admissions are the  highest by far in children age four and  under it's the same story in intensive  care units admissions are up across the  board but rates again are highest in  children age four and under doctors are  urging parents to take up the offer of  flu Jabs for their children so far  between 12 and 13 of two and three year  olds have been vaccinated but that's  well down on the same time last year  normally it's older adults who are most  likely to develop serious flu but  younger children are unlikely to have  any immunity to the virus because flu  rates have been negligible during the  covered shutdowns so what we're seeing  could be another hangover from the  pandemic
 

